# Routes a stylesheet edit to the instrument that proves it moved only what it meant to.
#
# ADR-018 sets the evidence bar for changing existing CSS, and `webview-visual-baseline`
# is what meets it. The reason it needs a hook rather than a doc: a CSS change that
# breaks a surface produces NO error anywhere. There are eight webview bundles and a
# feature stylesheet reaches only the ones whose entry imports it, so a class can be
# styled on one surface and absent on the next with nothing failing — CLAUDE.md calls
# this the single most common styling surprise in the repo.
rule_id=css-baseline
rule_once=1

rule_match() {
    case "$1" in
        Edit|Write) ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$3" ] || return 1
    case "$3" in
        */src/*.css) return 0 ;;
    esac
    return 1
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
You are changing a stylesheet. Before this edit, invoke the
`webview-visual-baseline` skill (Skill tool, skill: "webview-visual-baseline") and
capture a BEFORE fingerprint.

A CSS change that breaks a surface fails silently. Eight webview bundles exist and a
feature stylesheet reaches only the bundles whose entry imports it, so a class can be
styled on one surface and simply absent on the next with no error anywhere. The
computed-style fingerprint is what turns that into a diff you can read.

Three things the skill will stop you getting wrong, all measured:
  - `serve.sh` picks the port and the capture REFUSES to run without the sentinel —
    another app answering your port looks exactly like a broken instrument.
  - Run the capture against a build you have NOT changed before concluding anything.
  - `capture()` (RESTING) is the base; `captureInteractions()` is the supplement, and
    it now refuses until a resting capture has been recorded. On 2026-09-10 a day of
    CSS work was verified with the interaction half alone, and a dashboard regression
    — Spectrum's label padding eating 19px of a 96px tile once `box-sizing:
    border-box` began applying — reached a release spot-check. No hover, focus or
    active state can see a width change.

Every capture now records itself to `reports/visual-baseline/`, so "was a baseline
taken for this change?" is answerable afterwards. It was not, before.

This hook fires only once per session.
MSG
}
